  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nAn low pressure system moving south into northern California cut\noff by the northern stream will continue to lift northeast over\nthe next several days. Ahead of it, a departing upper ridge over\nthe Northern High Plains and southeast low level flow will\ncontinue with breezy conditions at times as well as the potential\nfor nocturnal or early morning patchy fog for some areas.\n\nLatest guidance is a bit further south with the storm track and \nshows a tight gradient in precipitation amounts over our CWA. As a\nresult, headlines appear unnecessary. Although, any embedded \nconvective elements that concentrate may raise totals locally. The\nNBM generally gives 0.5\" to 1.5\" of total QPF with the heaviest \ncorridor south of the Missouri River and west of the Yellowstone \nRiver. Lesser rainfall amounts are expected toward the Canadian \nborder and MT/ND border. Look for showers to become most \nwidespread Friday night and Saturday before the system pushes \ndownstream by Sunday. The Weather Prediction Center has \nhighlighted a marginal risk of excessive rainfall for Friday \nthrough Saturday.\n\nUpper ridging will yield drier and warmer conditions for early\nnext week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM and NBM90 were blended to nudge winds higher in advance of the\nupcoming low pressure system as southeast low level winds ramp up.\nMeanwhile, confidence is below average for this upcoming\nprecipitation event for Friday into Saturday given poor run to run\ncontinuity among the guidance, a trend toward the south, and a\ntight gradient over NE Montana. To that end, widespread rainfall\nranging from 0.25\" and 1.00\" are still largely most likely, with\nthe highest totals south of the Missouri River and west of the\nYellowstone River, concentrated over SW Phillips, Petroleum, and\nGarfield Counties.",
